window.docContents[139].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-colouring-blow-ups":{"contents":"A graph G is n-colourable iff every blow-up of G with nonempty parts is\nn-colourable. In one direction we pull a colouring back along the embedding\nEmbedding.blowUp of G into its blow-up (this is where we need the parts to be\nnonempty); in the other we compose with the projection Sigma.fst, which is a\nhomomorphism from the blow-up to G.\n\nlemma colorable_blowUpGraph_iff {n : ℕ} {ι : Type*} {W : ι → Type*}\n    (G : SimpleGraph ι) (f : ∀ i, W i) :\n    (G.blowUpGraph W).Colorable n ↔ G.Colorable n :=\n  ⟨fun h => h.of_hom (Embedding.blowUp G W f).toHom,\n   fun h => h.of_hom ⟨Sigma.fst, fun h => by simpa [blowUpGraph_adj] using h⟩⟩\n\n\nFor example, Mathlib's complete multipartite graph completeMultipartiteGraph W,\nwith parts W i, is by definition the blow-up of the complete graph on the index\ntype. So when there are finitely many parts, all nonempty, its chromatic number is\nthe number of parts.\n\nexample {ι : Type*} (W : ι → Type*) :\n    completeMultipartiteGraph W = (⊤ : SimpleGraph ι).blowUpGraph W := rfl\n\n\n#check completeMultipartiteGraph.chromaticNumber\n  -- [Fintype ι] (f : ∀ i, W i) : χ (completeMultipartiteGraph W) = Fintype.card ι\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Colouring Concrete Graphs","header":"7.5.1. Colouring Blow-ups","id":"/Colorings/#section-colouring-blow-ups"},"/Connectivity/#section-cut-vertices-and-blocks":{"contents":"A cut vertex is a vertex x whose removal splits the connected component\ncontaining x into two or more components. (For a finite graph this is the same as\nsaying that removing x increases the number of components, but that formulation\nfails for graphs with infinitely many components.)\n\nA block of a graph G is a subgraph B that is connected and has no cut vertex of\nits own, and that is maximal among such subgraphs: no larger subgraph of G is both\nconnected and free of cut vertices. So every block is of one of three kinds: a\nmaximal 2-connected subgraph, a bridge (the subgraph consisting of just that edge\nand its two endpoints), or an isolated vertex. One reason for caring about blocks is\nthat they can help with colouring a graph: for n ≥ 1, a graph is n-colourable iff\neach of its blocks is. In this section we prove the harder direction for finite\ngraphs (colorable_of_forall_block); the exercises give the converse and extend the\nresult to all graphs (colorable_iff_forall_block).\n\nA separate pedagogical motivation for introducing blocks is that it gives us a good\nexcuse to describe a graph-theoretic construction built from first principles using\nan equivalence relation and Lean's Quotient type.\n\nMathlib does not define cut vertices. In Lean the natural definition talks about\nwalks rather than components: x is a cut vertex if some two vertices other than x\nare joined by a walk in G, but every such walk passes through x.\n\nx is a cut vertex of G: some two vertices other than x are joined by a\nwalk, but every walk between them passes through x./-- x is a *cut vertex* of G: some two vertices other than x are joined by a\nwalk, but every walk between them passes through x. -/\ndef IsCutVertex (G : SimpleGraph V) (x : V) : Prop :=\n  ∃ u v, u ≠ x ∧ v ≠ x ∧ G.Reachable u v ∧ ∀ p : G.Walk u v, x ∈ p.support\n\n\nThis ties cut vertices to the vertex connectivity of\nVertex Connectivity. In the definition of IsVertexConnected 2\nthe deleted set S has at most one vertex. When S is empty the condition says that\nG is connected, and when S = {x} it says (for connected G) that x is not a\ncut vertex. So a graph is 2-vertex-connected iff it has at least three vertices, is\nconnected and has no cut vertex.\n\nlemma isVertexConnected_two_iff : G.IsVertexConnected 2 ↔\n    3 ≤ ENat.card V ∧ G.Connected ∧ ∀ x, ¬ G.IsCutVertex x := by\n  have h3 : (2 : ℕ∞) + 1 = 3 := by norm_num\n  constructor\n  · rintro ⟨hcard, h⟩\n    rw [h3] at hcard\n    have : Nonempty V :=\n      (ENat.card_ne_zero_iff_nonempty V).mp fun h0 => by\n        rw [h0] at hcard; norm_num at hcard\n    refine ⟨hcard, ⟨fun u v => ?_⟩, fun x ⟨u, v, hux, hvx, _, hp⟩ => ?_⟩\n    · -- deleting no vertices: G is connected\n      obtain ⟨p, -⟩ := h ∅ (by simp) u v (by simp) (by simp)\n      exact ⟨p⟩\n    · -- deleting x: some walk from u to v avoids x\n      obtain ⟨p, hp'⟩ :=\n        h {x} (by simp) u v (by simpa using hux) (by simpa using hvx)\n      exact hp' x (hp p) rfl\n  · rintro ⟨hcard, hconn, hcut⟩\n    refine ⟨h3 ▸ hcard, fun S hS u v hu hv => ?_⟩\n    -- a set of less than two vertices is empty or a single vertex x\n    rcases Set.encard_le_one_iff_eq.mp (by simpa using hS) with rfl | ⟨x, rfl⟩\n    · obtain ⟨p⟩ := hconn u v\n      exact ⟨p, by simp⟩\n    · by_contra! h\n      exact hcut x ⟨u, v, by grind, by grind, hconn u v, fun p => by grind [h p]⟩\n\n\nA cut vertex can lie in several blocks, so the blocks do not partition the vertices\nof a graph, but they do partition its edges. So we define blocks in the same way as\nconnected components, via an equivalence relation, but on edges rather than vertices.\nThe relation we use is cycle-equivalence: two edges are equivalent if they are the\nsame edge or lie in a common cycle. The equivalence classes of this relation will be\nthe blocks of the graph. A bridge lies on no cycles, so it forms a block on its own.\n\nApart from isolated vertices, the blocks in the sense defined at the start of this\nsection are exactly the subgraphs spanned by these equivalence classes, though we\nwill not prove this. An isolated vertex lies in no edge, so it belongs to no block in\nour sense; in particular a graph with no edges has no blocks at all. (This is why our\ncolouring results assume n ≥ 1: the graph K[1] has no blocks, so all its blocks\nare (vacuously) 0-colourable, but K[1] is not 0-colourable.)\n\nA graph with four blocks, its edges coloured by block. The cut vertices c and f lie in three and two blocks, and the isolated vertex i lies in no block\n\nLike the deleted sets in IsEdgeReachable, cycle-equivalence is most conveniently\ndefined on all of Sym2 V, not just on G.edgeSet. So until we restrict to\nG.edgeSet in Blocks as a Quotient of Edges, edge means any\nelement s(u, v) of Sym2 V, and we say edge of G for an element of\nG.edgeSet. An edge that is not an edge of G lies on no cycles, so it is\ncycle-equivalent only to itself.\n\nCycle-equivalence, a relation on Sym2 V: two edges are related if they are\nequal or lie on a common cycle./-- *Cycle-equivalence*, a relation on Sym2 V: two edges are related if they are\nequal or lie on a common cycle. -/\ndef CycleEquivalent (G : SimpleGraph V) : Sym2 V → Sym2 V → Prop :=\n  fun e₁ e₂ => e₁ = e₂ ∨\n    ∃ (u : V) (c : G.Walk u u), c.IsCycle ∧ e₁ ∈ c.edges ∧ e₂ ∈ c.edges\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.6. Cut Vertices and Blocks*","id":"/Connectivity/#section-cut-vertices-and-blocks"},"/Subgraphs/#section-subgraphs-as-simple-graphs":{"contents":"A G.Subgraph is not a SimpleGraph, so results about the latter do not\nautomatically apply to the former. In this section we look at how to pass between\nthe two: the coercions from a subgraph to a simple graph (and back), spanning\nsubgraphs, for which the two points of view essentially agree, and the parts of the\nSimpleGraph API that Mathlib duplicates for Subgraph.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs","header":"9.3. Subgraphs as Simple Graphs","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-subgraphs-as-simple-graphs"},"/Walks/#section-new-walks-from-old":{"contents":"We already saw how to concatenate two walks with append and how to use cons and\nconcat to add edges at the start and end of a walk. There are also a number of\nother ways to build new walks from old.\n\nSince edges in a SimpleGraph are undirected, we can reverse a walk p : G.Walk u v\nto get a walk p' : G.Walk v u.\n\n#check Walk.reverse --  (p : G.Walk u v) : G.Walk v u\n\n\nThe reversed walk has the same length as the original walk.\n\nexample (p : G.Walk u v) : p.reverse.length = p.length := Walk.length_reverse p\n\n\nGiven a vertex u that appears in a walk p : G.Walk v w, we can split the walk at\nthe first occurrence of u to get walks from v to u and from u to w.\n\n#check Walk.takeUntil  -- (p : G.Walk v w) (u : V) : u ∈ p.support → G.Walk v u\n\n\n#check Walk.dropUntil  -- (p : G.Walk v w) (u : V) : u ∈ p.support → G.Walk u w\n\n\nThese two walks can be concatenated to give back the original walk. (Note ++ is not\nactually defined for walks but is just useful informal notation for Walk.append.)\n\n#check Walk.take_spec -- p.takeUntil u h ++ p.dropUntil u h = p\n\n\nGiven a closed walk c₁ : G.Walk v v and a vertex u in its support, we can rotate\nthe walk to get a new closed walk c₂ : G.Walk u u (built as\ndropUntil ++ takeUntil).\n\n#check Walk.rotate  -- (c : G.Walk v v) (u : V) (h : u ∈ c.support) : G.Walk u u\n\n\nexample [DecidableEq V] (c : G.Walk v v) (h : u ∈ c.support) :\n  c.rotate u h = (c.dropUntil u h).append (c.takeUntil u h) := rfl\n\n\nUsing getVert we can also decompose walks by taking or dropping the first n\nedges, so that p.take n ends at the vertex p.getVert n.\n\n#check Walk.take --  (p : G.Walk u v) (n : ℕ) : G.Walk u (p.getVert n)\n#check Walk.drop --  (p : G.Walk u v) (n : ℕ) : G.Walk (p.getVert n) v\n#check Walk.append_take_drop_eq  -- p.take n ++ p.drop n = p\n\n\nSpecial cases of these include tail and dropLast.\n\n#check Walk.tail -- (p : G.Walk u v) : G.Walk p.snd v\n#check Walk.dropLast -- (p : G.Walk u v) : G.Walk u p.penultimate\n\n\nNotice that all of the walks defined using getVert have p.getVert n or similar in\ntheir type so, when there is a choice, the takeUntil, dropUntil or rotate walks\nmay be easier to work with. A vertex such as p.getVert n is often only\npropositionally (not definitionally) equal to the vertex we want, and the resulting\ntype mismatches are the subject of\nPlumbing: Fixing Type Mismatches.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.3. New Walks from Old","id":"/Walks/#section-new-walks-from-old"}});